      "card_ids": ["C09"],
      "claim": "Vistusertib, a high-transfer analogue with acidic and polar functionality, showed brain/plasma ratios below 5% in a steady-state mouse distribution study, indicating poor BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C03"],
      "claim": "The phosphorus-acid-containing analogue DHPP-2 showed extremely low brain uptake in rat whole-body autoradiography, directly supporting poor CNS access for the phosphinic-acid-containing query.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C04"],
      "claim": "Merck-3, a high-transfer analogue with hydroxyl, amine, and amide groups, was undetectable in mouse brain after IV dosing and was identified as a P-gp substrate, demonstrating efflux-limited poor brain penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C02"],
      "claim": "In humans, [11C]AcL703 showed low regional brain uptake by PET, providing direct human evidence that structurally related polar/heteroatom-rich molecules can have inadequate CNS access.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C08"],
      "claim": "In rats, [11C]3MPICA, which contains a carboxylic acid and amide, showed low brain uptake (0.11–0.19 %ID/g), supporting poor CNS penetration for the query's acidic/polar scaffold.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    },
    {
      "card_ids": ["C01"],
      "claim": "Neferine, a high-transfer analogue with methoxyphenyl and tertiary amine motifs, showed little brain distribution in rats, supporting that the query's methoxyphenyl/tertiary amine features do not overcome poor BBB penetration.",
      "evidence_role": "supportive"
    }
  ],
  "confidence": "high",
  "decision_summary": "Predicted fail: the query contains multiple ionizable/polar groups (phosphinic acid, tertiary amine, carboxylic acid) and all high-transfer experimental analogues show poor BBB penetration, including direct brain/plasma, brain uptake, and P-gp efflux evidence.",
